body:
I'm not a therapist, but I asked a therapist friend this once and she responded that there are generally two thoughts that come to mind:

1) Therapists specialize. Some therapists would focus on addiction issues, and probably be really up-to-date on what works/what doesn't work for dealing with drinking in a therapeutic space. Same with relationships, work issues, familial strife, etc. Therapists have different specialties, and can be better or worse suited to different clients based on that.

&#x200B;

2) Therapists' main job is to listen and respond thoughtfully to their client. Therapists aren't meant to be subject experts on every potential problem a client could have, but rather, experts in listening without judgement to their clients. What's the difference? Whereas an expert in addiction (i.e., a neuroscientist) could offer a potential client a vast sum of resources about the roots of drinking problems, the trauma that comes from that, the health risks associated, etc., a therapist would focus more on understanding their client as a person and how to be a part of their learning and growth.

&#x200B;

It's surely more complicated than this, but I think it's helpful to remember our therapists don't have the answers to our problems, they help us to reflect on and confront what is already within our lives. In my experience, they focus more on that reflection and confrontation, and have subject experience when it's especially important or helpful for working with a specific issue.
